.SHORT TERM...(Today through Friday)

A northward intrusion of mainly mid and high level tropical
moisture from the remnants of Marie continues through the North
Bay Area with more filling in overnight. Warm temperatures will
continue tonight with the incoming cloud layers greatly reducing
radiative cooling. Sub- cloud layer dry air does at times tend to
stay land- locked over the highly varied terrain of CA, until
diurnal mixing, diffusion, wind transport and/or precipitation get
going to thoroughly mix in the higher humidity to ground level.
The radar KMUX currently shows showers moving by about 70 miles
offshore. This bears close monitoring in the nearest term.

No changes to the forecast at this time. A Heat Advisory remains
in effect for the entire forecast area until 10 PM Thursday. Strong
high pressure will result in warm morning low temperatures and
daytime highs in the 90s to lower 100s over much of the forecast
area. Along the immediate coastline an afternoon sea breeze may
help nudge temperatures back down. This is a complex pattern,
please stay tuned for the latest updates. Cooler, more seasonal
temperatures are expected by Friday.

From the earlier forecast discussion:

Please remember these tips to help mitigate impacts from heat
exposure:

-Suspend unnecessary outdoor activities during the extreme heat, but
if you must be outdoors, take frequent breaks in the shade  or AC
and drink cool water.
-Know the signs and symptoms of Heat Exhaustion vs. Heat Stroke!
Heat Stroke can be deadly if not treated immediately by a medical
professional. Call 911 if you suspect someone is suffering from Heat
Stroke.
-Wear a life jacket, swim near a lifeguard, and check conditions
before entering the water if going to a river or ocean to cool off.
Rivers and the ocean are extremely cold, and Cold Water Shock can
turn into paralysis and drowning if you are not careful.
-Use a cool and damp towel on your body if you do not have AC and
are feeling hot. Or, take a cool shower or bath.
-Do not leave kids or pets in a parked car.
-Ensure pets and livestock have access to shade and water.

